The conversion of DME grows (from 31.7 to 54.6%) with an increase in pressure from 1.5 to 5.0 MPa, thereby reflecting the effect of growth in CO pressure on the rate of MA synthesis. A decrease in the selectivity for MA (from 3.4 to 0.7%) with the increasing pressure indicates that a high partial pressure of $\text{H}_2$ in the reaction mixture accelerates the hydrogenation of methyl acetate.

The formation of the alcohols is believed to occur through the initial carbonylation of DME in the H-MOR bed to give methyl acetate, the subsequent hydrogenation of which in the $\text{Cu/ZnO}$ bed results in methanol and ethanol as is shown below:

![Chemical reaction scheme: CO + DME leads to CH3COOCH3 (step 1), which then reacts with +H2 (step 2) to produce CH3OH + C2H5OH.]

$$
\begin{aligned}
\text{CO} + \text{DME} \xrightarrow{(1)} \text{CH}_3\text{COOCH}_3 \\n\xrightarrow[(2)]{+\text{H}_2} \text{CH}_3\text{OH} + \text{C}_2\text{H}_5\text{OH}
\end{aligned}
$$

Methanol can serve as a potential source of ethanol. Unconverted methanol can be easily separated

